categories:
- id: 1
  title: Deployment Environment
  description: Decisions regarding the hosting platform, serving method, and foundational
    architecture for the knowledge base.
  created_at: '2026-05-28T21:54:42'
  modified_at: '2026-05-28T21:54:42'
  decisions:
  - id: 1
    title: Use GitHub Pages as the Static Hosting Environment
    problem: The repository needs a free, reliable, and zero-maintenance hosting solution
      to serve technical articles globally. Initial ideas considered dynamic rendering
      (SSR).
    decision: Adopt a Static Site Generation (SSG) architecture and host the rendered
      HTML files on GitHub Pages. Dynamic SSR is abandoned for this specific use case.
    pros:
    - Zero hosting costs
    - Native integration with GitHub repositories
    - High availability and performance for static content
    cons:
    - Inability to run server-side logic (e.g., dynamic Jinja rendering on request)
      directly on the host
    modified_at: '2026-05-28T21:54:42'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  deprecated: false
- id: 2
  title: Documentation Engine & Core Plugins
  description: Decisions defining the framework, visual theme, and plugins used to
    parse Markdown and generate the static site.
  created_at: '2026-05-28T21:54:42'
  modified_at: '2026-05-28T21:54:43'
  decisions:
  - id: 1
    title: Use MkDocs with the Material Theme
    problem: Building and maintaining a custom markdown renderer requires significant
      effort and diverges focus from content creation.
    decision: Use MkDocs, a Python-based SSG, paired with the 'Material for MkDocs'
      theme.
    pros:
    - Industry standard for technical documentation
    - Out-of-the-box responsive design
    - Native support for tags and admonitions
    cons:
    - Requires learning the MkDocs configuration schema
    modified_at: '2026-05-28T21:54:42'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  - id: 2
    title: Implement mkdocs-static-i18n
    problem: The knowledge base must support multiple languages (English and Portuguese)
      with automatic URL synchronization between equivalent articles.
    decision: Use the mkdocs-static-i18n plugin to generate static language-specific
      versions of the site while maintaining a unified Markdown structure in the repository.
    pros:
    - Native language selector
    - SEO friendly
    - Transparent URL translation
    cons:
    - Adds minor complexity to the build process
    modified_at: '2026-05-28T21:54:42'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  - id: 3
    title: Implement mkdocs-macros-plugin
    problem: README.md files acting as directory index pages need to dynamically list
      all articles within their respective folders to avoid manual index updates.
    decision: Use the mkdocs-macros-plugin to inject Jinja2 templates directly into
      Markdown files, allowing dynamic iteration over page objects during the build.
    pros:
    - Eliminates manual index updates
    - Keeps logic inside standard Markdown files
    cons:
    - Markdown files containing Jinja syntax will display raw loops when viewed directly
      on GitHub's native viewer
    modified_at: '2026-05-28T21:54:43'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  deprecated: false
- id: 3
  title: Repository Structure and CI/CD
  description: Decisions regarding Git branching strategy and automated deployment
    pipelines.
  created_at: '2026-05-28T21:54:43'
  modified_at: '2026-05-28T21:54:43'
  decisions:
  - id: 1
    title: Branch Strategy
    problem: Maintaining all the HTML content in the same branch as the Markdown source
      files and configuration can lead to a terrible experience for users that want
      to clone the repository and view the Markdown files directly on GitHub or his
      own device.
    decision: Maintain a single 'main' branch containing both the Markdown content
      (docs/) and configuration files. Generated HTML will be isolated to the 'gh-pages'
      branch.
    pros:
    - Allows immediate local testing
    - Simplifies the Git workflow for content authors
    - Keeps main branch lightweight
    cons:
    - Mixes system configuration files with raw content in the same branch root
    modified_at: '2026-05-28T21:54:43'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  - id: 2
    title: Automate Build and Deployment via GitHub Actions
    problem: Manual compilation of the MkDocs site and subsequent pushing to the gh-pages
      branch is repetitive and prone to error.
    decision: Implement a GitHub Actions workflow triggered on pushes to the main
      branch to automatically install dependencies, build the static site, and deploy
      to gh-pages.
    pros:
    - Fully automated pipeline
    - Ensures production always reflects the main branch
    - No manual intervention required
    cons:
    - Relies on GitHub's CI/CD availability
    modified_at: '2026-05-28T21:54:43'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  deprecated: false
- id: 4
  title: Content Organization and Navigation
  description: Decisions regarding directory structure, metadata, and dynamic content
    generation for the knowledge base.
  created_at: '2026-05-28T21:54:43'
  modified_at: '2026-06-04T17:05:17'
  decisions:
  - id: 1
    title: Multilingual Directory Architecture
    problem: Content must be available in Portuguese and English, requiring a standardized
      structure that allows easy navigation and automatic URL synchronization.
    decision: Organize content using the strict pattern `docs/<lang>/<category>/[sub-category]/<file>.md`.
      Equivalent files must share the exact same filename across language directories.
    pros:
    - Clean separation of languages
    - Enables seamless language switching via the i18n plugin
    - Predictable path structure for content creators
    cons:
    - Requires discipline to maintain naming parity across the 'pt' and 'en' directories
    modified_at: '2026-05-28T21:54:43'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  - id: 2
    title: Invisible Metadata via YAML Front Matter
    problem: Articles need to be categorized and tagged for searchability and related-content
      linking without polluting the visual reading experience.
    decision: Embed tags and categories exclusively within standard YAML front matter
      at the top of each Markdown document. This header will be parsed by the engine
      but not rendered as text in the final HTML.
    pros:
    - Native support in MkDocs Material
    - Keeps the Markdown body clean
    - Enables automated aggregation and filtering
    cons:
    - Requires authors to follow a strict YAML schema for each post
    modified_at: '2026-05-28T21:54:43'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  - id: 3
    title: Dynamic Indexing
    problem: Maintaining manual lists of articles within categories and subcategories
      is error-prone and scales poorly as the repository grows.
    decision: Use the `README.md` files (rendered as `index.html` by MkDocs) from
      language|category|subcategory subdirectories as entrypoint for each one. Inject
      Jinja macros into these files to automatically iterate and list all child contents.
    pros:
    - Zero manual maintenance for category index pages
    - Preserves native GitHub navigation when reading raw files in the repository
    cons:
    - Raw Markdown files will display Jinja loop syntax when viewed directly on GitHub's
      native viewer
    modified_at: '2026-06-04T17:05:17'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  - id: 4
    title: Automated Content Showcases
    problem: The main landing page and individual articles need to surface relevant
      content to keep readers engaged, without requiring manual curation.
    decision: Utilize Jinja macros and blog plugins to automatically render the 3
      most recently published articles on the root `README.md`, and generate a simple
      list of up to 3 related articles based on shared tags at the bottom of each
      post.
    pros:
    - Improves user engagement and content discoverability
    - Fully automated layout updates whenever a new article is pushed
    cons:
    - Increases the processing overhead during the static site generation build phase
    modified_at: '2026-05-28T21:54:44'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  - id: 5
    title: Main Entry Point
    problem: We have different languages under `docs/`, a single README as root page
      would break the language selection.
    decision: 'Instead of using the repos''s root `README` as the landing page, we
      will place a `README.md` in each language subdirectory, redirecting who connects
      to `/` endpoint to its desired language, with fallback to English. E.g: `{url}/`
      → `{url}/en/` rendering the `docs/en/README.md` page.'
    pros: []
    cons: []
    modified_at: '2026-06-04T16:59:59'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  deprecated: false
- id: 5
  title: License
  description: Decisions defining the License structure and how they will be displayed.
  created_at: '2026-05-29T20:48:25'
  modified_at: '2026-06-04T16:10:01'
  decisions:
  - id: 1
    title: Source Code and Content Licenses
    problem: The Source Code from the html generator and the Content from the Articles
      have different natures.
    decision: Use MIT for the source code, and CC BY 4.0 for the content under `docs/`
      directory.
    pros: []
    cons: []
    modified_at: '2026-06-04T16:01:14'
    sub_decisions:
    - id: 1
      title: CC Copies
      problem: We should place a copy of the CC license under the `docs/` directory,
        but we have many languages in our structure.
      decision: 'Place a copy under each language subdirectory, e.g.: `docs/en/LICENSE.md`
        & `docs/pt/LICENSE.md`.'
      pros: []
      cons: []
      modified_at: '2026-06-04T15:54:46'
      deprecated: false
    deprecated: false
  - id: 2
    title: CC Notice on Pages
    problem: The CC note should appear on our content pages.
    decision: Our python script should place automatically a footer note in ALL the
      pages, with a link to the page `docs/{lang}/LICENSE.md` an to the official creative
      commons website `https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/`.
    pros: []
    cons: []
    modified_at: '2026-06-04T15:59:26'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  - id: 3
    title: LICENSE Pages
    problem: As the ADR-5.2 decides, we need to have a page inside our pages dedicated
      to the CC License copy.
    decision: Render the 'LICENSE' markdown placed at `docs/` - as decided on AR-5.1.1
      - under the endpoint `/{lang}/LICENSE`, in each laguage, as any other page,
      but without the footer CC notice.
    pros: []
    cons: []
    modified_at: '2026-06-04T16:06:16'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  - id: 4
    title: README Notice
    problem: The `README.md` is the "landing page" from our repository, we must add
      a License Notice declaring the scope of each one.
    decision: Write a "License" section, specifying the scopes from each LICENSE and
      link to them.
    pros: []
    cons: []
    modified_at: '2026-06-04T16:10:01'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
